INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('9d0cd579-7ee9-5ff0-b91d-52e945acb085', 'Philippines Sustainability Reporting Guidelines for Publicly Listed Companies', 'Sustainability Reporting Guidelines for Publicly Listed Companies', 'Issued by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) of the Philippines, these guidelines aim to enhance transparency and accountability in corporate sustainability reporting. The policy mandates publicly listed companies (PLCs) to disclose economic, environmental, social, and governance (EESG) performance, helping stakeholders assess their impact on sustainable development.

Key provisions include:

Mandatory Sustainability Reporting: PLCs must submit sustainability reports aligned with internationally recognized frameworks.
Materiality Assessment: Companies must identify and report on material sustainability issues.
Alignment with Global Standards: The guidelines integrate principles from:
Global Reporting Initiative (GRI) Standards
Sustainability Accounting Standards Board (SASB) Standards
Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD)
International Integrated Reporting Council (IIRC) Framework
Disclosure Requirements: Companies must report on:
Economic performance (revenue, local procurement, anti-corruption efforts).
Environmental impact (energy consumption, emissions, waste management).
Social impact (labor practices, diversity, customer engagement).
Governance measures (board oversight, risk management, stakeholder engagement).
Integration with United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (UN SDGs): Companies must link their disclosures to the UN SDGs and national sustainability targets.
The guidelines promote long-term business resilience, risk management, and investor confidence through structured sustainability reporting.', 'Issued by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) of the Philippines, these guidelines aim to enhance transparency and accountability in corporate sustainability reporting. The policy mandates publicly listed companies (PLCs) to disclose economic, environmental, social, and governance (EESG) performance, helping stakeholders assess their impact on sustainable development.

