Materiality Assessment: Companies must identify and report on material sustainability issues.
Alignment with Global Standards: The guidelines integrate principles from:
Global Reporting Initiative (GRI) Standards
Sustainability Accounting Standards Board (SASB) Standards
Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD)
International Integrated Reporting Council (IIRC) Framework
Disclosure Requirements: Companies must report on:
Economic performance (revenue, local procurement, anti-corruption efforts).
Environmental impact (energy consumption, emissions, waste management).
Social impact (labor practices, diversity, customer engagement).
Governance measures (board oversight, risk management, stakeholder engagement).
Integration with United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (UN SDGs): Companies must link their disclosures to the UN SDGs and national sustainability targets.
The guidelines promote long-term business resilience, risk management, and investor confidence through structured sustainability reporting.', 'Philippines', 'climate', 'in_force', '2019-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_6', ARRAY['guidance_frameworks', 'sdg13', 'sdg2', 'sdg7', 'sdg6', 'sdg16', 'sdg12', 'disclosure', 'philippines', 'voluntary']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('15fd8a8a-9b25-514c-9024-976ad299f04d', 'Philippines Administrative Order', 'Administrative Order No. 90-34', 'The DAO No. 90-34 is a revision of the water usage and classification or water quality criteria. Under the order is the water usage and classification of fresh surface waters, coastal and marine waters, the general provisions on water classification, the water quality criteria, the approved methods of analysis, as well as the significant parameters for selected types of industries. The order helps classify the varying bodies of water found in the Philippines. This is specially helpful with industries that would make use of bodies of water, such as the tourism industry or water services providers.', 'The DAO No. 90-34 is a revision of the water usage and classification or water quality criteria. Under the order is the water usage and classification of fresh surface waters, coastal and marine waters, the general provisions on water classification, the water quality criteria, the approved methods of analysis, as well as the significant parameters for selected types of industries. The order helps classify the varying bodies of water found in the Philippines. This is specially helpful with industries that would make use of bodies of water, such as the tourism industry or water services providers.', 'The DAO No. 90-34 is a revision of the water usage and classification or water quality criteria. Under the order is the water usage and classification of fresh surface waters, coastal and marine waters, the general provisions on water classification, the water quality criteria, the approved methods of analysis, as well as the significant parameters for selected types of industries. The order helps classify the varying bodies of water found in the Philippines. This is specially helpful with industries that would make use of bodies of water, such as the tourism industry or water services providers.', 'Philippines', 'nature', 'in_force', '2018-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_21', ARRAY['sdg14', 'other sustainability policy', 'mandatory', 'sdg6', 'mandatory_regulations', 'philippines', 'sdg9']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('f02db527-a27a-5a1b-8c59-7409123daec6', 'Corporation Code of the Philippines', 'Corporation Code of the Philippines', 'The Corporation Code of the Philippines discusses the following subjects: general provisions definitions and classifications; incorporation and organization of private corporations; board of directors/trustees and officers; general meetings; stocks and stockholders; corporate books and records; mergers and consolidation; appraisal; non-stock corporations (which includes: members, trustees and offices, and distribution of assets in non-stock corporations); close corporations; special corporations (which includes: educational corporations and religious corporations); dissolution; foreign corporations; and miscellaneous corporations. The Corporation Code provides the structure and guidelines needed to establish a corporation in the Philippines and also outlines governance procedures in various types of organizations, with accompanying reporting requirements.', 'The Corporation Code of the Philippines discusses the following subjects: general provisions definitions and classifications; incorporation and organization of private corporations; board of directors/trustees and officers; general meetings; stocks and stockholders; corporate books and records; mergers and consolidation; appraisal; non-stock corporations (which includes: members, trustees and offices, and distribution of assets in non-stock corporations); close corporations; special corporations (which includes: educational corporations and religious corporations); dissolution; foreign corporations; and miscellaneous corporations. The Corporation Code provides the structure and guidelines needed to establish a corporation in the Philippines and also outlines governance procedures in various types of organizations, with accompanying reporting requirements.', 'The Corporation Code of the Philippines discusses the following subjects: general provisions definitions and classifications; incorporation and organization of private corporations; board of directors/trustees and officers; general meetings; stocks and stockholders; corporate books and records; mergers and consolidation; appraisal; non-stock corporations (which includes: members, trustees and offices, and distribution of assets in non-stock corporations); close corporations; special corporations (which includes: educational corporations and religious corporations); dissolution; foreign corporations; and miscellaneous corporations. The Corporation Code provides the structure and guidelines needed to establish a corporation in the Philippines and also outlines governance procedures in various types of organizations, with accompanying reporting requirements.', 'Philippines', 'climate', 'in_force', '2018-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_22', ARRAY['sdg4', 'guidance_frameworks', 'mandatory', 'sdg16', 'disclosure', 'philippines']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('7994ba40-9bf5-5a41-bd10-3ad28f54e78e', 'Philippines DENR Administrative Order of', 'DENR Administrative Order No. 37 of 1996', 'DAO 1996-37 aims to further strengthen the implementation of the environmental impact statement (EIS) system using the following objectives: ensure that environmental, considerations are incorporated at the earliest possible stage of project development; further streamline the current procedures in the conduct of the Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) in order to improve its effectiveness as a planning, regulatory, and management tool; and enhance maximum participation in the EIA process to validate the social acceptability of the project or undertaking so as to ensure the fullest consideration of the environmental impact of such project or undertaking. The law covers the scope of the EIS system (coverage, non-coverage, EIS/IEE for Covered Projects or Undertakings, Environmental Safeguard for Projects or Undertakings Not Covered by EIS System), the procedural flow of the EIS System (Environmentally Critical Projects and Projects within Environmentally Critical Areas), Public Participation and Social Acceptability, Administrative Appeals, Roles and Responsibilities, schedule of fees, fines, penalties, and sanctions, transitory provisions, and the effectivity. The order tackles the topic of environmentally critical areas as well as the proper protocols in projects undertaken within said environmentally critical areas, such as the environmental impact statement or the initial environmental examination.', 'DAO 1996-37 aims to further strengthen the implementation of the environmental impact statement (EIS) system using the following objectives: ensure that environmental, considerations are incorporated at the earliest possible stage of project development; further streamline the current procedures in the conduct of the Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) in order to improve its effectiveness as a planning, regulatory, and management tool; and enhance maximum participation in the EIA process to validate the social acceptability of the project or undertaking so as to ensure the fullest consideration of the environmental impact of such project or undertaking. The law covers the scope of the EIS system (coverage, non-coverage, EIS/IEE for Covered Projects or Undertakings, Environmental Safeguard for Projects or Undertakings Not Covered by EIS System), the procedural flow of the EIS System (Environmentally Critical Projects and Projects within Environmentally Critical Areas), Public Participation and Social Acceptability, Administrative Appeals, Roles and Responsibilities, schedule of fees, fines, penalties, and sanctions, transitory provisions, and the effectivity. The order tackles the topic of environmentally critical areas as well as the proper protocols in projects undertaken within said environmentally critical areas, such as the environmental impact statement or the initial environmental examination.', 'DAO 1996-37 aims to further strengthen the implementation of the environmental impact statement (EIS) system using the following objectives: ensure that environmental, considerations are incorporated at the earliest possible stage of project development; further streamline the current procedures in the conduct of the Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) in order to improve its effectiveness as a planning, regulatory, and management tool; and enhance maximum participation in the EIA process to validate the social acceptability of the project or undertaking so as to ensure the fullest consideration of the environmental impact of such project or undertaking. The law covers the scope of the EIS system (coverage, non-coverage, EIS/IEE for Covered Projects or Undertakings, Environmental Safeguard for Projects or Undertakings Not Covered by EIS System), the procedural flow of the EIS System (Environmentally Critical Projects and Projects within Environmentally Critical Areas), Public Participation and Social Acceptability, Administrative Appeals, Roles and Responsibilities, schedule of fees, fines, penalties, and sanctions, transitory provisions, and the effectivity. The order tackles the topic of environmentally critical areas as well as the proper protocols in projects undertaken within said environmentally critical areas, such as the environmental impact statement or the initial environmental examination.', 'Philippines', 'governance', 'in_force', '2018-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_23', ARRAY['philippines', 'mandatory', 'regulations', 'disclosure']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('81d60648-3006-5ecb-91a7-c0c028e2ce52', 'Labor Code of the Philippines', 'Labor Code of the Philippines', 'The Labor Code''s objectives include the promotion and maintenance of a state of full employment through improved manpower, training, allocation, and utilization; the protection of every citizen desiring to work locally or overseas by securing for him the best possible terms and conditions of employment; the management of the free choice of available employment by persons seeking work in conformity with the national interest; the management and regulation of the movement of workers in conformity with the national interest; the supervision of the employment of aliens, including the establishment of a registration and/or work permit system; the strengthening of the network of public employment offices and the rationalization of the participation of the private sector in the recruitment and placement of workers, locally and overseas, to serve national objectives; and the careful selection of Filipino workers for overseas employment in order to protect the good name of the Philippines abroad. The Labor Code provides requirements for registration for labor foreigners and labor organizations.', 'The Labor Code''s objectives include the promotion and maintenance of a state of full employment through improved manpower, training, allocation, and utilization; the protection of every citizen desiring to work locally or overseas by securing for him the best possible terms and conditions of employment; the management of the free choice of available employment by persons seeking work in conformity with the national interest; the management and regulation of the movement of workers in conformity with the national interest; the supervision of the employment of aliens, including the establishment of a registration and/or work permit system; the strengthening of the network of public employment offices and the rationalization of the participation of the private sector in the recruitment and placement of workers, locally and overseas, to serve national objectives; and the careful selection of Filipino workers for overseas employment in order to protect the good name of the Philippines abroad. The Labor Code provides requirements for registration for labor foreigners and labor organizations.', 'The Labor Code''s objectives include the promotion and maintenance of a state of full employment through improved manpower, training, allocation, and utilization; the protection of every citizen desiring to work locally or overseas by securing for him the best possible terms and conditions of employment; the management of the free choice of available employment by persons seeking work in conformity with the national interest; the management and regulation of the movement of workers in conformity with the national interest; the supervision of the employment of aliens, including the establishment of a registration and/or work permit system; the strengthening of the network of public employment offices and the rationalization of the participation of the private sector in the recruitment and placement of workers, locally and overseas, to serve national objectives; and the careful selection of Filipino workers for overseas employment in order to protect the good name of the Philippines abroad. The Labor Code provides requirements for registration for labor foreigners and labor organizations.', 'Philippines', 'nature', 'in_force', '2017-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_26', ARRAY['sdg4', 'sdg8', 'sdg14', 'other sustainability policy', 'regulations', 'mandatory', 'philippines']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('d1913580-6e7b-5f80-a2e7-e2a4667f2c43', 'Philippines Administrative Order', 'Administrative Order No. 04-08', 'The DAO No. 04-08 revises DAO 2000-18 and DAO 2002-22 and establishes a new Chemical Control Order for Ozone Depleting Substances. Found in the order is the submission of the following provisions: the requirements for importer registration and the pre-shipment importation registration for ODS and the requirements for the registration of dealers, retailers, and resellers of ODS. The new Order establishes the ban on importation of Ozone Depleting Substances; phases-out schedule and controls of importation of ODS; discusses the registration of importers and application of pre-shipment importation clearance for ODS, the registration of dealers, retailers, and re-sellers of ODS; the certification of Service Providers and Accreditation of Service Shops of ODS-Using Equipment; discusses the keeping of records; discusses the topic of confidential business information; establishes prohibited acts; and finally, the penalties and repealing clauses.', 'The DAO No. 04-08 revises DAO 2000-18 and DAO 2002-22 and establishes a new Chemical Control Order for Ozone Depleting Substances. Found in the order is the submission of the following provisions: the requirements for importer registration and the pre-shipment importation registration for ODS and the requirements for the registration of dealers, retailers, and resellers of ODS. The new Order establishes the ban on importation of Ozone Depleting Substances; phases-out schedule and controls of importation of ODS; discusses the registration of importers and application of pre-shipment importation clearance for ODS, the registration of dealers, retailers, and re-sellers of ODS; the certification of Service Providers and Accreditation of Service Shops of ODS-Using Equipment; discusses the keeping of records; discusses the topic of confidential business information; establishes prohibited acts; and finally, the penalties and repealing clauses.', 'The DAO No. 04-08 revises DAO 2000-18 and DAO 2002-22 and establishes a new Chemical Control Order for Ozone Depleting Substances. Found in the order is the submission of the following provisions: the requirements for importer registration and the pre-shipment importation registration for ODS and the requirements for the registration of dealers, retailers, and resellers of ODS. The new Order establishes the ban on importation of Ozone Depleting Substances; phases-out schedule and controls of importation of ODS; discusses the registration of importers and application of pre-shipment importation clearance for ODS, the registration of dealers, retailers, and re-sellers of ODS; the certification of Service Providers and Accreditation of Service Shops of ODS-Using Equipment; discusses the keeping of records; discusses the topic of confidential business information; establishes prohibited acts; and finally, the penalties and repealing clauses.', 'Philippines', 'climate', 'in_force', '2016-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_20', ARRAY['philippines', 'mandatory', 'other sustainability policy', 'regulations']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('0ba0d206-5c20-55b5-91be-a7f9305f2fd0', 'Philippines Philippine Green Jobs Act of', 'Philippine Green Jobs Act of 2016', 'The Philippine Green Jobs Act aims to promote the creation of green jobs in various sectors, including agriculture, fisheries, forestry, and waste management', 'The Philippine Green Jobs Act aims to promote the creation of green jobs in various sectors, including agriculture, fisheries, forestry, and waste management', 'The Philippine Green Jobs Act aims to promote the creation of green jobs in various sectors, including agriculture, fisheries, forestry, and waste management', 'Philippines', 'nature', 'in_force', '2016-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_32', ARRAY['sdg2', 'sdg14', 'other sustainability policy', 'regulations', 'mandatory', 'sdg12', 'philippines', 'sdg15']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('86b12c42-51ba-50cc-b903-1392067d42da', 'Philippines Philippine Corporate Governance Blueprint', 'Philippine Corporate Governance Blueprint 2015', 'The cover design for the Philippine Corporate Governance Blueprint 2015 represents the overarching ideals of the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) to promote fairness, transparency and accountability. The color green aligns with the SEC’s color palette while also symbolizing the concepts of growth, renewal, balance and sustainability – representing the SEC’s continuing efforts to nurture healthy and vibrant corporate environments and capital markets in the Philippines. The different waves moving up and outward from the SEC logo signify the interconnections among the various regulatory bodies, companies and other stakeholders and how these groups can flow seamlessly together to create a structured corporate governance framework for the country. The facets in the background represent the multiple angles, perspectives and insights that have contributed to the completion of the CG Blueprint.', 'The cover design for the Philippine Corporate Governance Blueprint 2015 represents the overarching ideals of the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) to promote fairness, transparency and accountability. The color green aligns with the SEC’s color palette while also symbolizing the concepts of growth, renewal, balance and sustainability – representing the SEC’s continuing efforts to nurture healthy and vibrant corporate environments and capital markets in the Philippines. The different waves moving up and outward from the SEC logo signify the interconnections among the various regulatory bodies, companies and other stakeholders and how these groups can flow seamlessly together to create a structured corporate governance framework for the country. The facets in the background represent the multiple angles, perspectives and insights that have contributed to the completion of the CG Blueprint.', 'The cover design for the Philippine Corporate Governance Blueprint 2015 represents the overarching ideals of the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) to promote fairness, transparency and accountability. The color green aligns with the SEC’s color palette while also symbolizing the concepts of growth, renewal, balance and sustainability – representing the SEC’s continuing efforts to nurture healthy and vibrant corporate environments and capital markets in the Philippines. The different waves moving up and outward from the SEC logo signify the interconnections among the various regulatory bodies, companies and other stakeholders and how these groups can flow seamlessly together to create a structured corporate governance framework for the country. The facets in the background represent the multiple angles, perspectives and insights that have contributed to the completion of the CG Blueprint.', 'Philippines', 'nature', 'in_force', '2015-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_1', ARRAY['sdg14', 'mandatory', 'sdg16', 'mandatory_regulations', 'disclosure', 'philippines', 'sdg3']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('852895fe-96cf-5df1-9e19-146e30b1dc38', 'Philippines Committe Report . An Act institutionalizing corporate social', 'Committe Report No. 22. An Act institutionalizing corporate social responsibility, providing incentives therefor, and for other purposes', 'This document is concerned with an act that instutionalizes corporate social responsibility providing incentives therefor and for other purposes.', 'This document is concerned with an act that instutionalizes corporate social responsibility providing incentives therefor and for other purposes.', 'This document is concerned with an act that instutionalizes corporate social responsibility providing incentives therefor and for other purposes.', 'Philippines', 'climate', 'in_force', '2011-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_2', ARRAY['philippines', 'mandatory', 'other sustainability policy', 'regulations']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('1def57db-b552-5bca-bdc1-b0c962f3c47e', 'Philippines Corporate Governance Guidelines for Companies Listed on the', 'The Corporate Governance Guidelines for Companies Listed on the Philippine Stock Exchange', 'The Guidelines aim to clarify and present the corporate governance standards to which the PSE believes all well-governed publicly-listed companies adopt. This was developed after a careful review and assessment of internationally recognized corporate governance codes and best practices as well as a rigorous local and regional stakeholder engagement process. The PSE believes that the standards reflected in the Guidelines do not only conform to international best practices, but are also relevant and responsive to the Philippine business environment and culture.', 'The Guidelines aim to clarify and present the corporate governance standards to which the PSE believes all well-governed publicly-listed companies adopt. This was developed after a careful review and assessment of internationally recognized corporate governance codes and best practices as well as a rigorous local and regional stakeholder engagement process. The PSE believes that the standards reflected in the Guidelines do not only conform to international best practices, but are also relevant and responsive to the Philippine business environment and culture.', 'The Guidelines aim to clarify and present the corporate governance standards to which the PSE believes all well-governed publicly-listed companies adopt. This was developed after a careful review and assessment of internationally recognized corporate governance codes and best practices as well as a rigorous local and regional stakeholder engagement process. The PSE believes that the standards reflected in the Guidelines do not only conform to international best practices, but are also relevant and responsive to the Philippine business environment and culture.', 'Philippines', 'governance', 'in_force', '2010-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_4', ARRAY['guidance_frameworks', 'sdg17', 'sdg16', 'disclosure', 'philippines', 'voluntary']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('a7e0f615-f61b-5a7f-bd9d-9294c3aeb3c0', 'Philippines Revised Code of Corporate Governance', 'Revised Code of Corporate Governance, 2009', 'Pursuant to its mandate under the Securities Regulation Code and the Corporation Code, the Securities and Exchange Commission (the “Commission”), in a meeting held on June 18, 2009, approved the promulgation of this Revised Code of Corporate Governance (the “Code”) which shall apply to registered corporations and to branches or subsidiaries of foreign corporations operating in the Philippines that (a) sell equity and/or debt securities to the public that are required to be registered with the Commission, or (b) have assets in excess of Fifty Million Pesos and at least two hundred (200) stockholders who own at least one hundred (100) shares each of equity securities, or (c) whose equity securities are listed on an Exchange; or (d) are grantees of secondary licenses from the Commission.', 'Pursuant to its mandate under the Securities Regulation Code and the Corporation Code, the Securities and Exchange Commission (the “Commission”), in a meeting held on June 18, 2009, approved the promulgation of this Revised Code of Corporate Governance (the “Code”) which shall apply to registered corporations and to branches or subsidiaries of foreign corporations operating in the Philippines that (a) sell equity and/or debt securities to the public that are required to be registered with the Commission, or (b) have assets in excess of Fifty Million Pesos and at least two hundred (200) stockholders who own at least one hundred (100) shares each of equity securities, or (c) whose equity securities are listed on an Exchange; or (d) are grantees of secondary licenses from the Commission.', 'Pursuant to its mandate under the Securities Regulation Code and the Corporation Code, the Securities and Exchange Commission (the “Commission”), in a meeting held on June 18, 2009, approved the promulgation of this Revised Code of Corporate Governance (the “Code”) which shall apply to registered corporations and to branches or subsidiaries of foreign corporations operating in the Philippines that (a) sell equity and/or debt securities to the public that are required to be registered with the Commission, or (b) have assets in excess of Fifty Million Pesos and at least two hundred (200) stockholders who own at least one hundred (100) shares each of equity securities, or (c) whose equity securities are listed on an Exchange; or (d) are grantees of secondary licenses from the Commission.', 'Philippines', 'governance', 'in_force', '2009-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_5', ARRAY['regulations', 'mandatory', 'sdg16', 'disclosure', 'philippines']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('de353766-83c1-5ada-92fa-4ab1046be62e', 'Philippines Republic Act - Credit Information System Act (CISA)', 'Republic Act No. 9510 - Credit Information System Act (CISA)', 'Republic Act 9510 establishes the Credit Information System with the expectation of: greatly improving the overall availability of credit especially to micro, small, and medium-scale enterprises; providing mechanisms to make credit more cost-effective; and reducing the excessive dependence on collateral to secure credit facilities. The scope of the act includes: the establishment of the Credit Information System, the establishment of the Central Credit Information Corporation, the confidentiality of Credit Information, the Educational Campaign, the rules and regulations, the congressional oversight committee, and the indemnity in favor of the corporation, its officers and employees. The credit information system caters specifically towards the financial industry.', 'Republic Act 9510 establishes the Credit Information System with the expectation of: greatly improving the overall availability of credit especially to micro, small, and medium-scale enterprises; providing mechanisms to make credit more cost-effective; and reducing the excessive dependence on collateral to secure credit facilities. The scope of the act includes: the establishment of the Credit Information System, the establishment of the Central Credit Information Corporation, the confidentiality of Credit Information, the Educational Campaign, the rules and regulations, the congressional oversight committee, and the indemnity in favor of the corporation, its officers and employees. The credit information system caters specifically towards the financial industry.', 'Republic Act 9510 establishes the Credit Information System with the expectation of: greatly improving the overall availability of credit especially to micro, small, and medium-scale enterprises; providing mechanisms to make credit more cost-effective; and reducing the excessive dependence on collateral to secure credit facilities. The scope of the act includes: the establishment of the Credit Information System, the establishment of the Central Credit Information Corporation, the confidentiality of Credit Information, the Educational Campaign, the rules and regulations, the congressional oversight committee, and the indemnity in favor of the corporation, its officers and employees. The credit information system caters specifically towards the financial industry.', 'Philippines', 'governance', 'in_force', '2008-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_28', ARRAY['sdg4', 'other sustainability policy', 'regulations', 'mandatory', 'philippines', 'sdg9']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('e619c83e-3aec-5030-a020-06a1a681236c', 'Philippines Revised Procedural Manual for DENR Administrative Order No.30', 'Revised Procedural Manual for DENR Administrative Order No.30 Series of 2003', 'This revised version of the manual has three (3) major features: 1) Integration of new EMB policies on application, review, decision-making and public participation, including explicit instructions on how to consider EIA within the project planning cycle as well as how to situate the EIA process in relation to issues and concerns outside the mandate of the DENR- EMB; 2) Standardization and shortening of procedures from EIA Scoping to EIA Review, and flowcharting of the EIA application procedures across EIA Report types, project groupings and across EMB offices (CO or RO) for more efficient EIA processes and easy tracking of status of applications; 3) Condensation and revised formatting of EIA Reports and Decision Documents, and inclusion of more matrices, checklists, outlines, pro-forma documents and specific guidelines to focus EIA Study Terms of Reference, EIA Reports, Review and Evaluation, Decision Making, and Monitoring, Validation and Evaluation/Audit to the essentials.', 'This revised version of the manual has three (3) major features: 1) Integration of new EMB policies on application, review, decision-making and public participation, including explicit instructions on how to consider EIA within the project planning cycle as well as how to situate the EIA process in relation to issues and concerns outside the mandate of the DENR- EMB; 2) Standardization and shortening of procedures from EIA Scoping to EIA Review, and flowcharting of the EIA application procedures across EIA Report types, project groupings and across EMB offices (CO or RO) for more efficient EIA processes and easy tracking of status of applications; 3) Condensation and revised formatting of EIA Reports and Decision Documents, and inclusion of more matrices, checklists, outlines, pro-forma documents and specific guidelines to focus EIA Study Terms of Reference, EIA Reports, Review and Evaluation, Decision Making, and Monitoring, Validation and Evaluation/Audit to the essentials.', 'This revised version of the manual has three (3) major features: 1) Integration of new EMB policies on application, review, decision-making and public participation, including explicit instructions on how to consider EIA within the project planning cycle as well as how to situate the EIA process in relation to issues and concerns outside the mandate of the DENR- EMB; 2) Standardization and shortening of procedures from EIA Scoping to EIA Review, and flowcharting of the EIA application procedures across EIA Report types, project groupings and across EMB offices (CO or RO) for more efficient EIA processes and easy tracking of status of applications; 3) Condensation and revised formatting of EIA Reports and Decision Documents, and inclusion of more matrices, checklists, outlines, pro-forma documents and specific guidelines to focus EIA Study Terms of Reference, EIA Reports, Review and Evaluation, Decision Making, and Monitoring, Validation and Evaluation/Audit to the essentials.', 'Philippines', 'climate', 'in_force', '2007-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_16', ARRAY['sdg10', 'mandatory', 'mandatory_regulations', 'disclosure', 'philippines']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('ef93aec3-b94f-5050-b7d6-5144e3a74294', 'Philippines Securitization Act of', 'The Securitization Act of 2004', 'The Securitization of Act of 2004 provides the regulatory framework for securitization and granting for the purpose of exemptions from the operation of certain laws. Discussed in the Act are the following topics: special purpose entity, the servicer, tax and other related issues, secondary mortgage institution, rating system, and penal and miscellaneous provisions. The Securitization act also requires the servicer to submit periodic reports.', 'The Securitization of Act of 2004 provides the regulatory framework for securitization and granting for the purpose of exemptions from the operation of certain laws. Discussed in the Act are the following topics: special purpose entity, the servicer, tax and other related issues, secondary mortgage institution, rating system, and penal and miscellaneous provisions. The Securitization act also requires the servicer to submit periodic reports.', 'The Securitization of Act of 2004 provides the regulatory framework for securitization and granting for the purpose of exemptions from the operation of certain laws. Discussed in the Act are the following topics: special purpose entity, the servicer, tax and other related issues, secondary mortgage institution, rating system, and penal and miscellaneous provisions. The Securitization act also requires the servicer to submit periodic reports.', 'Philippines', 'climate', 'in_force', '2007-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_27', ARRAY['philippines', 'mandatory', 'other sustainability policy', 'regulations']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('1987fbf8-709b-534d-b153-40ad1b2e0aa2', 'Philippines act to direct the use of biofuels establishing', 'An act to direct the use of biofuels establishing for this purpose the biofuel program, appropriating funds therefor and for other purposes', 'An act to direct the use of biofuels, establishing for this purpose the bIofuel program, appropluating funds therefor, and for other purposes.', 'An act to direct the use of biofuels, establishing for this purpose the bIofuel program, appropluating funds therefor, and for other purposes.', 'An act to direct the use of biofuels, establishing for this purpose the bIofuel program, appropluating funds therefor, and for other purposes.', 'Philippines', 'governance', 'in_force', '2006-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_17', ARRAY['philippines', 'mandatory', 'other sustainability policy', 'regulations']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('6be5aef2-6f1d-5878-855d-ae4035c3abef', 'Philippines Republic Act or the "Biofuels Act of 2006"', 'Republic Act No. 9367 or the "Biofuels Act of 2006"', 'The Biofuels Act of 2006 was created with the aim of: developing and utilizing renewable and sustainability-sourced clean energy sources; mitigating toxic and greenhouse gas emissions; and ensuring the  availability of alternative and renewable clean energy. The energy industry is specifically affected here where they have to submit provisions for the blending, production, and distribution of biofuels. The act discusses the Phasing Out of the Use of Harmful Gasoline Additives and/or Oxygenates, the Mandatory Use of Biofuels, the Incentive Scheme to encourage investments, the Powers and Functions of the Department of Energy, the Creation, Powers, and Functions of the National Biofuel Board, the Security of Domestic Sugar Supply, the Role of the Government Agencies, Prohibited Acts, Penal Provisions, Appropriations, the Implementation of the Rules and Regulations, the Congressional Oversight Committee, and the Benefits of Biofuel Workers.', 'The Biofuels Act of 2006 was created with the aim of: developing and utilizing renewable and sustainability-sourced clean energy sources; mitigating toxic and greenhouse gas emissions; and ensuring the  availability of alternative and renewable clean energy. The energy industry is specifically affected here where they have to submit provisions for the blending, production, and distribution of biofuels. The act discusses the Phasing Out of the Use of Harmful Gasoline Additives and/or Oxygenates, the Mandatory Use of Biofuels, the Incentive Scheme to encourage investments, the Powers and Functions of the Department of Energy, the Creation, Powers, and Functions of the National Biofuel Board, the Security of Domestic Sugar Supply, the Role of the Government Agencies, Prohibited Acts, Penal Provisions, Appropriations, the Implementation of the Rules and Regulations, the Congressional Oversight Committee, and the Benefits of Biofuel Workers.', 'The Biofuels Act of 2006 was created with the aim of: developing and utilizing renewable and sustainability-sourced clean energy sources; mitigating toxic and greenhouse gas emissions; and ensuring the  availability of alternative and renewable clean energy. The energy industry is specifically affected here where they have to submit provisions for the blending, production, and distribution of biofuels. The act discusses the Phasing Out of the Use of Harmful Gasoline Additives and/or Oxygenates, the Mandatory Use of Biofuels, the Incentive Scheme to encourage investments, the Powers and Functions of the Department of Energy, the Creation, Powers, and Functions of the National Biofuel Board, the Security of Domestic Sugar Supply, the Role of the Government Agencies, Prohibited Acts, Penal Provisions, Appropriations, the Implementation of the Rules and Regulations, the Congressional Oversight Committee, and the Benefits of Biofuel Workers.', 'Philippines', 'climate', 'in_force', '2006-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_29', ARRAY['sdg8', 'sdg13', 'other sustainability policy', 'regulations', 'sdg7', 'mandatory', 'sdg12', 'philippines', 'sdg9']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('63703a69-f5e0-5129-a4a8-9709fa0fd675', 'Philippines Securities and Exchange Commission Financial Disclosures Checklist', 'Securities and Exchange Commission Financial Disclosures Checklist', 'This checklist summarizes the disclosures required by SRC Rules 68 and 68.1, current SFAS/IAS in effect as of January 1, 2004, except SFAS 19 and 19A, standards related to the banking industry. The checklist does not explain the other accounting requirements of the SFAS/IAS. In some instances, to simplify use of the checklist, disclosure requirements have been paraphrased, therefore if necessary, reference should be made to the standards themselves for full details.', 'This checklist summarizes the disclosures required by SRC Rules 68 and 68.1, current SFAS/IAS in effect as of January 1, 2004, except SFAS 19 and 19A, standards related to the banking industry. The checklist does not explain the other accounting requirements of the SFAS/IAS. In some instances, to simplify use of the checklist, disclosure requirements have been paraphrased, therefore if necessary, reference should be made to the standards themselves for full details.', 'This checklist summarizes the disclosures required by SRC Rules 68 and 68.1, current SFAS/IAS in effect as of January 1, 2004, except SFAS 19 and 19A, standards related to the banking industry. The checklist does not explain the other accounting requirements of the SFAS/IAS. In some instances, to simplify use of the checklist, disclosure requirements have been paraphrased, therefore if necessary, reference should be made to the standards themselves for full details.', 'Philippines', 'governance', 'in_force', '2004-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_3', ARRAY['mandatory', 'mandatory_regulations', 'disclosure', 'philippines', 'sdg9']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('31d89f50-ad6a-5189-bb49-8886384fc04e', 'Philippines Code of Corporate Governance', 'Code of Corporate Governance, 2002', 'In accordance with the State’s policy to actively promote corporate governance reforms aimed to raise investor confidence, develop capital market and help achieve high sustained growth for the corporate sector and the economy, the Commission, in its Resolution No.135, Series of 2002 dated April 04 2002, approved the promulgation and implementation of this Code, which shall be applicable to corporations whose securities are registered or listed, corporations which are grantees of permits/licenses and secondary franchise from the Commission and public companies. This Code also applies to branches or subsidiaries of foreign corporations operating in the Philippines whose securities are registered or listed.', 'In accordance with the State’s policy to actively promote corporate governance reforms aimed to raise investor confidence, develop capital market and help achieve high sustained growth for the corporate sector and the economy, the Commission, in its Resolution No.135, Series of 2002 dated April 04 2002, approved the promulgation and implementation of this Code, which shall be applicable to corporations whose securities are registered or listed, corporations which are grantees of permits/licenses and secondary franchise from the Commission and public companies. This Code also applies to branches or subsidiaries of foreign corporations operating in the Philippines whose securities are registered or listed.', 'In accordance with the State’s policy to actively promote corporate governance reforms aimed to raise investor confidence, develop capital market and help achieve high sustained growth for the corporate sector and the economy, the Commission, in its Resolution No.135, Series of 2002 dated April 04 2002, approved the promulgation and implementation of this Code, which shall be applicable to corporations whose securities are registered or listed, corporations which are grantees of permits/licenses and secondary franchise from the Commission and public companies. This Code also applies to branches or subsidiaries of foreign corporations operating in the Philippines whose securities are registered or listed.', 'Philippines', 'governance', 'in_force', '2002-01-01'::date, 'Philippines', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/144_7', ARRAY['regulations', 'mandatory', 'sdg16', 'disclosure', 'philippines']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
